=== END ===

The errors had one cause. `Flow<U, V, S>` is invariant in `V`, because `loop`'s `body` and `until` are function-typed properties that take and return `Flow<U, V, S>`. So `Flow<X, Review, S>` can never be assigned to `Flow<X, unknown, S>`.

Three surface signatures used `unknown` as a wildcard for "any value type": `Lane.handle`, `Flow.when` and `Flow.parallel`. Each now carries its own type parameter instead. Nothing is loosened, and the scenarios are unchanged.

- **`Lane.handle`** is now `handle<V>(flow: Flow<T, V, void>)` and `handle<V, S>(flow: Flow<T, V, S>, settings: S)`. A lane accepts a handler whatever its final value type is.
- **`Flow.when`** is now `when<W>(predicate, then: (flow: Flow<U, V, S>) => Flow<U, W, S>)`. The branch's value is inferred, not widened to `unknown`.
- **`Flow.parallel`** now infers branch values through a mapped type: `parallel<B>(name, branches: { [K in keyof B]: (flow) => Flow<U, B[K], S> }): Flow<U, { readonly [K in keyof B]: B[K] }, S>`. This also fixes the merge step in scenario 6. Its input was collapsing to `{ [x: string]: never }` because `FlowValue` matched on `Flow<never, …, unknown>`, which never matches an invariant `Flow`.
- **`FlowValue`** is removed from the surface, since nothing uses it any more.

